smoltcp: a loopback interface beside a NIC's, sharing the sockets

netd runs two interfaces over one socket set: the NIC's, and a loopback
one for 127.0.0.0/8 and the host's own address (a TCP listener on
0.0.0.0 then answers both). Upstream, each interface sends what it can
route: the NIC's would send 127.0.0.1 to the default router, the
loopback one would take broadcasts and remote destinations, and a packet
an interface cannot route silences its socket as if a neighbor were
missing. set_local_only() makes an interface send only to its own
addresses and prefixes (and leave the DHCP sockets alone: it has no
hardware address to ask with), set_local_elsewhere() makes the other one
leave those (and 127.0.0.0/8) alone, and a packet neither of a pair
routes waits in its socket for the other interface. Unchanged without
either. With tests (tests/ipv4.rs). Derived from smoltcp, so under
smoltcp's license (0BSD).

